  private mode throws on access, so every storage call is wrapped and degrades to in-memory for the page's lifetime
  rather than breaking login.

  The constraint I'll write into the spec: [1mthis id is for tracking who logged in, not for deciding what they may do.[0m It
  is client-readable and client-writable; any script on the page can change it. Authorization stays server-side against
  a real session. I'm recording it because a stored user id is exactly the kind of thing that later gets read as "the
  current user is allowed to…".

  [1m5. Error handling[0m

  [38;5;153mlogin[39m rejects on network failure; the handler catches, logs, and leaves the existing session untouched — a failed
  login must not clear a good id. A successful response missing [38;5;153muserId[39m is treated as a server error, logged, and does
  not overwrite storage.

  [1m6. Testing[0m

  There is no test runner, no lint config, and no scripts in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m — the project has no configured tooling at
  all. That's the cheapest thing to fix right now, before any of this code exists, so it's part of the design rather
  than an afterthought.

